            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous Thrombosis of a Left Circumflex Artery Fistula Draining Into the Coronary Sinus.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>640-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/2150135115577432</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 28-year-old woman was referred to our institution with sudden onset chest pain at rest. Electrocardiography revealed an inferoposterior ST-segment elevation myocardial infraction. The patient proceeded to cardiac catheterization, which revealed a large ectatic left circumflex coronary artery with an apparent fistula connecting it to the coronary sinus. Follow-up angiography revealed that the fistula was no longer patent, spontaneously thrombosed, and resolved without requiring surgery.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© The Author(s) 2015.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
